(Unit 5: Indexing) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An index is an auxiliary structure that speeds up data retrieval at the cost of extra) Tj T*
(storage and slower writes. A B+ tree index keeps keys sorted in a balanced tree whose leaves) Tj T*
(are linked, so it supports both equality lookups and range queries efficiently in O\(log n\)) Tj T*
(time. A hash index maps keys to buckets with a hash function and is very fast for equality) Tj T*
(searches but cannot answer range queries.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A clustered index determines the physical order of rows in the table, so a table can have) Tj T*
(only one clustered index. A non-clustered \(secondary\) index stores pointers to rows and a) Tj T*
(table may have many. Choosing indexes requires balancing query performance against insert) Tj T*
(and update overhead.) Tj T*
ET
